  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(275.16pt, 752.64pt, "Page ")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(302.52pt, 752.64pt, "3")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(308.88pt, 752.64pt, " of ")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(324pt, 752.64pt, "13")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72pt, 331.92pt, "Figure 1: A Schwarz-P surface is a primitive triply periodic minimal surface, defined by zero mean ")
  #line-at(72pt, 347.76pt, "curvature and periodicity in three spatial directions. Mirroring the basic unit in three planes creates ")
  #line-at(72pt, 363.72pt, "the presented object, which we refer to as a unit cell. Interestingly, the unit cell intersects with a ")
  #line-at(72pt, 379.56pt, "bounding cube at three circles - a property that we will leverage in this work for construction of ")
  #line-at(72pt, 395.4pt, "the object by ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(136.92pt, 395.4pt, "LiquiFab.")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72pt, 425.28pt, "SIMULATION TOOL ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72pt, 449.16pt, "The Schwarz-P surface is a mathematical minimal surface of zero thickness (Figure 1). ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#242424"))
  #line-at(519.12pt, 449.16pt, "Any ")
  #line-at(72pt, 465pt, "physical realization possesses finite volume and is therefore an approximation of the ideal ")
  #line-at(72pt, 480.84pt, "geometry")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(116.52pt, 480.84pt, ". ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(125.28pt, 480.84pt, "LiquiFab")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(170.04pt, 480.84pt, " treats fabrication as a constrained minimum-energy problem: the liquid ")
  #line-at(72pt, 496.8pt, "interface satisfies prescribed boundaries, while the remaining surface relaxes to its minimal-energy ")
  #line-at(72pt, 512.64pt, "configuration.")
  #line-at(72pt, 536.52pt, "To approximate the Schwarz P topology, we define six annular rings placed on the faces of a cube ")
  #line-at(72pt, 552.36pt, "(Figure 2b). These rings act as fixed boundary curves defining the geometric constraints for the ")
  #line-at(72pt, 568.2pt, "minimum-energy problem. To compute the shape of the object, we use Surface Evolver (Brakke, ")
  #line-at(72pt, 584.04pt, "1992), which we have integrated into the Rhino/Grasshopper environment.  ")
  #line-at(72pt, 607.92pt, "The user defines the cube dimensions together with lists of the inner and outer radii of the rings ")
  #line-at(72pt, 623.88pt, "that are to be located at each face (Figure 2a). Based on these inputs, our Grasshopper script ")
  #line-at(72pt, 639.72pt, "generates the geometric entities required by Surface Evolver, including the boundary curves and ")
  #line-at(72pt, 655.56pt, "surfaces and an initial watertight mesh (Figures 2b–e). Once the initial mesh is constructed, it ")
  #line-at(72pt, 671.4pt, "undergoes a remeshing procedure intended to standardize element quality and accelerate ")
  #line-at(72pt, 687.36pt, "convergence. Surface Evolver then iteratively adjusts vertex positions to minimize the total surface ")
  #line-at(72pt, 703.2pt, "energy while preserving the prescribed boundary constraints and the initial volume enclosed by ")
]

