#original-page(612pt, 792pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(275.16pt, 752.64pt, "Page ")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(302.52pt, 752.64pt, "5")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(308.88pt, 752.64pt, " of ")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(324pt, 752.64pt, "13")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72pt, 83.16pt, "volume needed for construction is essential. We envision a space habitat, whose envelope is ")
  #line-at(72pt, 99pt, "constructed entirely in space from the assembly of individual LiquiBricks, each on the scale of ")
  #line-at(72pt, 114.84pt, "meters to tens of meters.  ")
  #line-at(72pt, 144.72pt, "The classical Schwarz-P unit cell provides an appealing starting point for such applications: it ")
  #line-at(72pt, 160.68pt, "encloses space efficiently with minimal surface area and connecting several units in series ")
  #line-at(72pt, 176.52pt, "naturally forms interconnected passages. However, defining the geometry through boundary ")
  #line-at(72pt, 192.36pt, "elements - rather than prescribing the minimal surface explicitly - opens up a richer design space. ")
  #line-at(72pt, 208.2pt, "This boundary-driven approach allows the creation of Schwarz-P-inspired surfaces that preserve ")
  #line-at(72pt, 224.16pt, "the beneficial characteristics of minimal surfaces while relaxing strict triply periodic symmetry. In ")
  #line-at(72pt, 240pt, "this section we use our computational model to demonstrate how variations in boundary conditions ")
  #line-at(72pt, 255.84pt, "yield diverse LiquiBrick units that can be assembled into larger architectural systems.  ")
  #line-at(72pt, 285.72pt, "In addition to boundary geometry, we also investigate the role of the ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(412.56pt, 285.72pt, "liquid volume")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(479.16pt, 285.72pt, " used during ")
  #line-at(72pt, 301.56pt, "fabrication. Adjusting this parameter directly influences how material distributes itself within the ")
  #line-at(72pt, 317.4pt, "resulting shell - yielding structures that range from thin, lightweight envelopes to thicker, more ")
  #line-at(72pt, 333.36pt, "robust walls - while still conforming to the minimal-energy configuration imposed by the ")
  #line-at(72pt, 349.2pt, "boundaries. Together, these parameters define a coherent and versatile design-to-fabrication ")
  #line-at(72pt, 365.04pt, "strategy for generating space-relevant minimal-surface structures. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72pt, 394.92pt, "Boundary elements ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72pt, 418.8pt, "Figure 3 illustrates how the geometry of a ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(308.16pt, 418.8pt, "LiquiBrick")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(359.64pt, 418.8pt, " can be systematically shaped by ")
  #line-at(72pt, 434.64pt, "manipulating the boundary conditions that define it. The sequence begins with a reference ")
  #line-at(72pt, 450.48pt, "configuration (Figure 3a), consisting of six annular rings arranged symmetrically on the faces of a ")
  #line-at(72pt, 466.44pt, "cube. This base form provides a controlled starting point from which several derivative geometries ")
  #line-at(72pt, 482.28pt, "are produced. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72pt, 506.16pt, "Variation in ring diameter. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(219.36pt, 506.16pt, "The first family of variations (Figure 3b) explores the effect of ")
  #line-at(72pt, 522pt, "increasing the nominal diameter of the boundary rings while keeping their positions fixed. ")
  #line-at(72pt, 537.84pt, "Enlarging these diameters changes two essential characteristics. First, the resulting openings ")
  #line-at(72pt, 553.8pt, "between adjacent rings become wider, which is crucial in space-structure applications where ")
  #line-at(72pt, 569.64pt, "internal circulation of air, transfer of equipment, cable routing, or crew access must be ")
  #line-at(72pt, 585.48pt, "accommodated. Second, increasing the ring diameter reduces the amount of liquid required to ")
  #line-at(72pt, 601.32pt, "“close the surface” - that is, the volume of polymer needed to create a continuous minimal-energy ")
  #line-at(72pt, 617.16pt, "interface spanning all boundary rings.  ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72pt, 641.04pt, "Variation in ring thickness. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(214.56pt, 641.04pt, "The second set of variations (Figure 3c) adjusts the thickness of the ")
  #line-at(72pt, 657pt, "boundary rings - that is, the width of the annular face to which the liquid surface attaches. This ")
  #line-at(72pt, 672.84pt, "face can be made narrow c(i) or wide c(ii), providing a simple geometric control over the extent ")
  #line-at(72pt, 688.68pt, "of the contact region. Ring thickness therefore governs how each unit interfaces with adjacent ")
]

// arXiv source page 6
#original-page(612pt, 792pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(275.16pt, 752.64pt, "Page ")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(302.52pt, 752.64pt, "6")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(308.88pt, 752.64pt, " of ")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(324pt, 752.64pt, "13")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72pt, 83.16pt, "components, enabling the design of broader docking areas, reinforced attachment zones, or more ")
  #line-at(72pt, 99pt, "compact connection points. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72pt, 122.88pt, "Combined and customized configurations. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(301.92pt, 122.88pt, "The third series (Figure 3d) demonstrates how ")
  #line-at(72pt, 138.72pt, "variations in diameter and thickness can be combined to create highly customized boundary ")
  #line-at(72pt, 154.68pt, "configurations. These examples show that even non-uniform or asymmetric rings reliably generate ")
  #line-at(72pt, 170.52pt, "coherent minimal-surface geometries. This flexibility enables the encoding of functional ")
  #line-at(72pt, 186.36pt, "distinctions directly into the boundary layout.  ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72pt, 210.24pt, "Assembly of multiple units. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(213.36pt, 210.24pt, "Figure 3e demonstrates that individual LiquiBricks can be combined ")
  #line-at(72pt, 226.08pt, "into larger assemblies by aligning their boundary configurations. Different unit types - such as ")
  #line-at(72pt, 241.92pt, "those derived from figure 3d(i) and figure 3d(iv) - can be arranged to create structures that ")
  #line-at(72pt, 257.88pt, "accommodate various functional requirements in a modular way. Here, the continuity of openings, ")
  #line-at(72pt, 273.72pt, "alignment of interface regions, and compatibility of local curvatures all emerge naturally from the ")
  #line-at(72pt, 289.56pt, "boundary-driven design process. ")
  #line-at(72pt, 313.44pt, "Together, the variations illustrated in figure 3 demonstrate a versatile design space in which ")
  #line-at(72pt, 329.28pt, "inter-module compatibility arises from deliberate control of boundary geometry. ")
]

