// arXiv source page 12
#original-page(612pt, 792pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(271.92pt, 752.64pt, "Page ")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(299.28pt, 752.64pt, "12")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(312pt, 752.64pt, " of ")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(327.12pt, 752.64pt, "13")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72pt, 83.16pt, "DISCUSSION AND CONSIDERATIONS FOR IMPLEMNTATION ON THE ISS ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72pt, 107.04pt, "This paper presents a design-to-fabrication workflow for generating minimal-surface structures ")
  #line-at(72pt, 122.88pt, "through fluid-interface self-organization in microgravity. By integrating simulation, parametric ")
  #line-at(72pt, 138.72pt, "design, and experimental validation, the research demonstrates how boundary conditions, ")
  #line-at(72pt, 154.68pt, "influencing a physical process, can be used to drive form generation. ")
  #line-at(72pt, 178.56pt, "To date, our experiments were conducted in a neutral buoyancy environment, simulating ")
  #line-at(72pt, 194.4pt, "microgravity conditions. A true microgravity environment presents new challenges, but at the ")
  #line-at(72pt, 210.24pt, "same time also eliminates some of the constraints of neutral buoyancy. As we have learned from ")
  #line-at(72pt, 226.08pt, "prior in-space manufacturing experiments, the precise behavior of a complex system in space ")
  #line-at(72pt, 241.92pt, "cannot always be precisely anticipated. (Ericson et al., 2026) To learn more about the behavior of ")
  #line-at(72pt, 257.88pt, "our system in space, we are planning an experiment on board the international space station ")
  #line-at(72pt, 273.72pt, "(currently scheduled for the first quarter of 2027) in which we intend to produce a Schwarz-P ")
  #line-at(72pt, 289.56pt, "object in space and return the fabricated objects back to Earth for further analysis.   ")
  #line-at(72pt, 313.44pt, "One constraint that is relaxed in space is the choice of polymer. While on Earth the polymer must ")
  #line-at(72pt, 329.28pt, "be insoluble in water and within a specific density range, operation in true microgravity allows the ")
  #line-at(72pt, 345.24pt, "use of essentially any polymer. While the experiments we presented in this paper were performed ")
  #line-at(72pt, 361.08pt, "using a simple polyurethane that cannot be considered as a structural material in space, other ")
  #line-at(72pt, 376.92pt, "polymer such as Dur")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 8.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(171.96pt, 372.36pt, "TM")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(184.08pt, 376.92pt, " 173S (Grossman et al., 2024) have been shown to be compatible with the ")
  #line-at(72pt, 392.76pt, "conditions in space, and can be directly used with the LiquiFab approach.  ")
  #line-at(72pt, 416.64pt, "While the immersion liquid provides conditions in which the total body force on the liquid is zero, ")
  #line-at(72pt, 432.48pt, "just as in microgravity, the significant liquid mass around the object likely offers additional ")
  #line-at(72pt, 448.44pt, "stabilization that may not exist in space. Furthermore, while on Earth the polymer is inflated with ")
  #line-at(72pt, 464.28pt, "immersion liquid, in space it will be inflated with gas. And finally - the film puncturing dynamics ")
  #line-at(72pt, 480.12pt, "may be significantly different in the absence of an immersion liquid. In other words, while the ")
  #line-at(72pt, 495.96pt, "steady-state shape should be identical on Earth and in space, liquid dynamics may be quite different ")
  #line-at(72pt, 511.92pt, "and can be experimentally investigated only in space.  ")
  #line-at(72pt, 535.8pt, "An additional difference between Earth and Space experiments relates to thermal management. On ")
  #line-at(72pt, 551.64pt, "Earth, free convection, particularly in the presence of the immersion liquid, plays a critical role in ")
  #line-at(72pt, 567.48pt, "dissipating heat generated during the exothermic polymerization process. In microgravity, ")
  #line-at(72pt, 583.32pt, "however, the absence of buoyancy-driven convection limits passive heat transfer. As a result, active ")
  #line-at(72pt, 599.16pt, "cooling (e.g. by forced convection) must be implemented to maintain stable material properties.  ")
  #line-at(72pt, 623.04pt, "Successful implementation of the method in space would validate the feasibility of the approach. ")
  #line-at(72pt, 639pt, "Since the method is inherently scale-invariant, i.e. the physical behavior and resulting shapes are ")
  #line-at(72pt, 654.84pt, "independent of size, extrapolation to large scale is straightforward. Naturally, additional design ")
  #line-at(72pt, 670.68pt, "and engineering research would be required for such manufacturing at a large scale.  ")
]

